      "role": "user",
      "content": "the idea of something \"near-dormant\" or \"energy-based\" is getting into territory that's hard to reconcile with typical farm management. it's also very frustrating because the more we look for a scientific or mechanical cause, the more the actual physical evidence contradicts it. \n\nwe've even set up high-speed cameras to try and catch a glimpse of anything during these \"nocturnal shifts,\" but we just get footage of empty, silent aisles. then we come back in the morning and there's another set of these perfect little bites. it's like they aren't just hiding, they're actually *mocking* the security protocols. \n\nhonestly, my team is starting to joke that we've got some kind of tiny, invisible saboteurs in here. it's reaching a point where it feels less like a biological infestation and more like some kind of... well, like a household nuisance or a pest that is specifically designed to be a nuisance and stay out of sight. is there any known biological behavior that is purely, fundamentally about being a persistent, untraceable nuisance to a larger host?",
